= Solution
{parent=a}

Write
$$
q(v)=\sqrt{f'(v)^2+g'(v)^2}.
$$
The regularity of the profile makes $q>0$. The coordinate tangent vectors are
$$
\phi_u=(-f\sin u,f\cos u,0),
\qquad
\phi_v=(f'\cos u,f'\sin u,g'),
$$
so the <first fundamental form of a surface of revolution> has coefficients
$$
E=f^2,
\qquad F=0,
\qquad G=q^2.
$$
Choose the <unit normal>
$$
N=\frac1q(-g'\cos u,-g'\sin u,f').
$$
Taking scalar products with the second derivatives of $\phi$ gives the <second fundamental form> coefficients
$$
e=\frac{fg'}q,
\qquad f_{\mathrm{II}}=0,
\qquad g_{\mathrm{II}}=\frac{f'g''-g'f''}q.
$$
Thus the coordinate directions are <principal direction>[principal directions], with <principal curvature>[principal curvatures]
$$
k_1=\frac{g'}{fq},
\qquad
k_2=\frac{f'g''-g'f''}{q^3}.
$$
The <curvatures of a parametrized surface of revolution> are therefore
$$
\boxed{H=\frac12\left(\frac{g'}{fq}+\frac{f'g''-g'f''}{q^3}\right),
\qquad
K=\frac{g'(f'g''-g'f'')}{fq^4}.}
$$
Reversing the <orientation of a surface> reverses the sign of $H$ but leaves $K$ unchanged.

= b
{parent=25h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

A regular surface is a <minimal surface> when its <mean curvature> vanishes identically, equivalently when its two <principal curvature>[principal curvatures] sum to zero everywhere.

The <catenoid>
$$
\phi(u,v)=(\cosh v\cos u,\cosh v\sin u,v)
$$
is an example. In part (a), take $f(v)=\cosh v$ and $g(v)=v$, so $q=\cosh v$. The formula there gives
$$
H=\frac12\left(\operatorname{sech}^2v-\operatorname{sech}^2v\right)=0,
\qquad
K=-\operatorname{sech}^4v<0.
$$
A plane and a <circular cylinder> have zero <Gaussian curvature>, as does a <flat cone> away from its vertex. Because a <local isometry> preserves Gaussian curvature, **the catenoid is minimal and is not locally isometric to any of those surfaces**; this is the <catenoid is a minimal surface>.

= c
{parent=25h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

**No.** Fix $0<A<1$ and, near $s=0$, let
$$
f(s)=A\cos s,
\qquad
g(s)=\int_0^s\sqrt{1-A^2\sin^2t}\,dt.
$$
Then $f'^2+g'^2=1$, so this is a regular unit-speed profile with $f>0$ on a sufficiently small interval. Rotate it around the $z$-axis. For an arc-length <surface of revolution>, $K=-f''/f$, and therefore
$$
K=-\frac{-A\cos s}{A\cos s}=1.
$$
At $s=0$, the formulas from part (a) give the <principal curvature>[principal curvatures]
$$
k_1=\frac1A,
\qquad k_2=A.
$$
They are unequal because $A\ne1$, so the point is not an <umbilical point>. Every point of a sphere is umbilical. Consequently no neighbourhood of this point can lie on a sphere, giving a <non-spherical surface of constant Gaussian curvature one>.

= 26K
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Probability and Measure}

= a
{parent=26k}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Assume $X_n\to X$ in $L^2$. The reverse <triangle inequality> for the $L^2$ norm gives
$$
\left|\lVert X_n\rVert_2-\lVert X\rVert_2\right|
\leq\lVert X_n-X\rVert_2\longrightarrow0.
$$
Squaring the norms yields
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[X_n^2]\longrightarrow\mathbb E[X^2].}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Now assume $X_n\to X$ <almost surely> and $\mathbb E[X_n^2]\to\mathbb E[X^2]$. In particular, $(X_n)$ is bounded in $L^2$, so it is <uniform integrability from bounded second moments>[uniformly integrable] in $L^1$. Almost-sure convergence and <uniform integrability> therefore give
$$
\mathbb E|X_n-X|\longrightarrow0.
$$

We next show that $X_n$ converges weakly to $X$ in the <L2 space>. For $Y\in L^2$, let $Y_M=Y\mathbf1_{\{|Y|\leq M\}}$. Then
$$
|\mathbb E[(X_n-X)Y_M]|\leq M\mathbb E|X_n-X|\longrightarrow0,
$$
while the <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> gives, uniformly in $n$,
$$
|\mathbb E[(X_n-X)(Y-Y_M)]|
\leq(\lVert X_n\rVert_2+\lVert X\rVert_2)\lVert Y-Y_M\rVert_2.
$$
Letting first $n\to\infty$ and then $M\to\infty$ proves $\mathbb E[X_nY]\to\mathbb E[XY]$. Taking $Y=X$ and expanding the square now gives
$$
\mathbb E[(X_n-X)^2]
=\mathbb E[X_n^2]+\mathbb E[X^2]-2\mathbb E[X_nX]
\longrightarrow0.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{X_n\longrightarrow X\text{ in }L^2.}
$$
This proves the <almost-sure convergence and convergence of second moments> criterion.

= b
{parent=26k}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <quantile function> $X_\mu=F_\mu^{-1}$ is nondecreasing, hence a <Borel measurable function>. More explicitly, for every $x\in\mathbb R$,
$$
\{\omega\in(0,1):X_\mu(\omega)\leq x\}
=(0,F_\mu(x)]\cap(0,1).
$$
Thus $X_\mu$ is a <random variable>, and because Lebesgue measure is being used on $(0,1)$,
$$
\mathbb P(X_\mu\leq x)=F_\mu(x).
$$
Its <cumulative distribution function> is therefore $F_\mu$, so
$$
\boxed{\mathcal L(X_\mu)=\mu.}
$$
This is <inverse transform sampling>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Suppose first that
$$
\mathbb E[(X_{\mu_n}-X_\nu)^2]\longrightarrow0.
$$
This is <convergence in L2>, hence <convergence in probability>, which implies <convergence in distribution>. By part (i), the respective laws are $\mu_n$ and $\nu$, so $\mu_n$ converges <weak convergence of probability measures>[weakly] to $\nu$. Part (a)(i) also gives
$$
\int_{\mathbb R}x^2\,d\mu_n(x)
=\mathbb E[X_{\mu_n}^2]
\longrightarrow
\mathbb E[X_\nu^2]
=\int_{\mathbb R}x^2\,d\nu(x).
$$

Conversely, suppose that $\mu_n$ converges weakly to $\nu$ and the displayed second moments converge. By the stated <quantile coupling for convergence in distribution>,
$$
X_{\mu_n}\longrightarrow X_\nu
\qquad\text{almost surely}.
$$
Part (i) identifies their second moments with those of the measures, so part (a)(ii) applies and gives convergence in $L^2$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{
\mathbb E[(X_{\mu_n}-X_\nu)^2]\to0
\iff
\left(\mu_n\Rightarrow\nu
\text{ and }
\int x^2\,d\mu_n\to\int x^2\,d\nu\right).}
$$
This is the <one-dimensional second-Wasserstein convergence criterion> realized by the common-quantile coupling.

= 27K
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Applied Probability R}

= a
{parent=27k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For every <Borel set> $A\subseteq\mathbb R^d$, let
$$
N(A)=\#(\Pi\cap A)
$$
be its random point count. A <Non-homogeneous Poisson point process> with <intensity function of a point process> $\lambda$ is characterized by:

* $N(A)$ is finite almost surely for every bounded Borel set $A$;
* for every bounded Borel set $A$,
$$
N(A)\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\Lambda(A)),
\qquad
\Lambda(A)=\int_A\lambda(x)\,dx;
$$
* the counts $N(A_1),\ldots,N(A_m)$ are <independent random variables> whenever $A_1,\ldots,A_m$ are pairwise disjoint bounded Borel sets.

Because $\Lambda$ has a density with respect to <Lebesgue measure>, it is atomless, so the process is simple: almost surely no location occurs more than once.

= b
{parent=27k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <mapping theorem for Poisson point processes> applies provided $f$ is a <measurable function> and its <pushforward measure>
$$
f_*\Lambda(B)=\Lambda(f^{-1}(B))
$$
is locally finite, meaning
$$
\Lambda(f^{-1}(B))<\infty
$$
for every bounded Borel set $B\subseteq\mathbb R^s$. If “Poisson process” means a simple point process, one also requires $f_*\Lambda$ to be atomless, equivalently
$$
\Lambda(f^{-1}(\{y\}))=0
\qquad(y\in\mathbb R^s).
$$
Under these conditions, $f(\Pi)$ is a <Poisson point process> with mean measure
$$
\boxed{\Lambda_f(B)=f_*\Lambda(B)=\Lambda(f^{-1}(B)).}
$$

= c
{parent=27k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Here $\Lambda$ is planar <Lebesgue measure>. For a Borel set $B\subseteq[0,\infty)$, <polar coordinates> give
$$
\begin{aligned}
f_*\Lambda(B)
&=\int_{\mathbb R^2}\mathbf1_B(x_1^2+x_2^2)\,dx_1\,dx_2\\
&=\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^\infty\mathbf1_B(r^2)r\,dr\,d\theta\\
&=\pi\int_Bdt.
\end{aligned}
$$
The pushforward is therefore locally finite and atomless. By the <mapping theorem for Poisson point processes>, $f(\Pi)$ is a homogeneous <Poisson process> on $[0,\infty)$ with **constant intensity $\pi$**. This is the two-dimensional <radial volume transform of a homogeneous Poisson point process>.

Put $S_k=R_k^2$. The variable $S_k$ is the $k$th arrival time of that rate-$\pi$ Poisson process, hence has the <gamma distribution> with shape $k$ and rate $\pi$:
$$
g_k(s)=\frac{\pi^k}{(k-1)!}s^{k-1}e^{-\pi s},
\qquad s>0.
$$
Since $s=r^2$ and $ds/dr=2r$, the <change-of-variables formula for a probability density> gives the <Kth-nearest-neighbour distance in a homogeneous Poisson point process>
$$
\boxed{h_k(r)=2r\,g_k(r^2)
=\frac{2\pi r(\pi r^2)^{k-1}e^{-\pi r^2}}{(k-1)!},
\qquad r>0.}
$$

= 28J
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Principles of Statistics}

= a
{parent=28j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For observations $x=(x_1,\ldots,x_n)$, the <likelihood function> is
$$
L(\theta;x)=\prod_{i=1}^nf(x_i;\theta).
$$
A <maximum-likelihood estimator> is any measurable choice
$$
\boxed{\widehat\theta_{\mathrm{MLE}}(x)\in\operatorname*{argmax}_{\theta\in\Theta}L(\theta;x).}
$$
For one observation in a regular scalar model, the <score function> is
$$
S_\theta(X)=\frac{\partial}{\partial\theta}\log f(X;\theta),
$$
and the <Fisher information> is
$$
\boxed{I(\theta)=\mathbb E_\theta[S_\theta(X)^2]
=-\mathbb E_\theta\left[\frac{\partial^2}{\partial\theta^2}\log f(X;\theta)\right],}
$$
where the second identity requires the usual differentiation-under-the-integral regularity. For $n$ independent observations, <tensorization of Fisher information> gives $I_n(\theta)=nI(\theta)$.

= b
{parent=28j}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <strong law of large numbers> gives
$$
\boxed{\widehat\mu_n=\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^nh(X_i)
\longrightarrow\mathbb E_{\theta_0}[h(X)]=\mu(\theta_0)
\quad\text{almost surely}.}
$$
A continuous one-to-one real function is strictly monotone and has a continuous inverse on its image. Hence
$$
\boxed{\widehat\theta_n=\mu^{-1}(\widehat\mu_n)}
$$
whenever $\widehat\mu_n$ lies in that image; an arbitrary extension outside the image handles the remaining finite-sample values. The <continuous mapping theorem> gives $\widehat\theta_n\to\theta_0$ almost surely, so this is a <consistent estimator>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Put
$$
\sigma_h^2=\operatorname{Var}_{\theta_0}(h(X)).
$$
The <central limit theorem> gives $\sqrt n(\widehat\mu_n-\mu(\theta_0))\Rightarrow N(0,\sigma_h^2)$. Provided $\mu'(\theta_0)\ne0$, the <delta method> applied to $\mu^{-1}$ yields
$$
\boxed{\sqrt n(\widehat\theta_n-\theta_0)
\xrightarrow d
N\left(0,\frac{\sigma_h^2}{\mu'(\theta_0)^2}\right).}
$$
Strict monotonicity alone does not exclude $\mu'(\theta_0)=0$; in that exceptional case this root-$n$ limit need not hold and higher-order analysis is required.

One does **not** necessarily have $\operatorname{Var}(\widehat\theta_n)\geq[nI(\theta_0)]^{-1}$ for every $n$, because $\widehat\theta_n$ need not be unbiased. The <Cramer-Rao bound> for an estimator with bias $B_n(\theta)$ is instead
$$
\operatorname{Var}_\theta(\widehat\theta_n)
\geq\frac{(1+B_n'(\theta))^2}{nI(\theta)}
$$
under the corresponding regularity assumptions.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For each $i$, let $\widehat\theta_{(-i)}$ be the same estimator computed after deleting $X_i$, and put $\overline\theta_{(-)}=n^{-1}\sum_i\widehat\theta_{(-i)}$. The <jackknife bias correction> is
$$
\boxed{\widehat\theta_{\mathrm J}
=n\widehat\theta_n-(n-1)\overline\theta_{(-)}.}
$$
Its bias is
$$
nB_n(\theta_0)-(n-1)B_{n-1}(\theta_0)=O(n^{-2}),
$$
so the leading $a/n$ term has been removed and the bias is smaller than that of $\widehat\theta_n$.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The assumed <maximum-likelihood estimator> is the sample mean of $h(X_i)$. Its <central limit theorem> is
$$
\sqrt n(\widehat\theta_{\mathrm{MLE}}-\theta_0)
\xrightarrow d N\left(0,\operatorname{Var}_{\theta_0}(h(X))\right).
$$
For a regular model, the regular maximum-likelihood limit is
$$
\sqrt n(\widehat\theta_{\mathrm{MLE}}-\theta_0)
\xrightarrow d N(0,I(\theta_0)^{-1}).
$$
The two asymptotic variances must agree, so
$$
\boxed{I(\theta_0)=\frac1{\operatorname{Var}_{\theta_0}(h(X))}.}
$$
Consequently
$$
\operatorname{Var}(\widehat\theta_{\mathrm{MLE}})
=\frac{\operatorname{Var}(h(X))}{n}
=\frac1{nI(\theta_0)}
$$
to first asymptotic order. The estimator therefore attains the <Cramer-Rao bound> asymptotically: its precision, the reciprocal variance, exhausts the information in the sample.

= 29K
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Stochastic Financial Models}

= a
{parent=29k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

In the one-period building block of the <Cox--Ross--Rubinstein model>, the money-market account grows by a deterministic factor $R>0$, while the stock grows independently at each time by
$$
Y_t=\begin{cases}U,&\text{up},\\D,&\text{down},\end{cases}
\qquad 0<D<U.
$$
Thus $S_t^0=R^tS_0^0$ and $S_t^1=S_0^1\prod_{j=1}^tY_j$. Equivalently, the discounted stock has multipliers $u=U/R$ and $d=D/R$.

The model has no <arbitrage> precisely when the risk-free return lies strictly between the stock returns:
$$
\boxed{D<R<U,\qquad\text{equivalently}\qquad d<1<u.}
$$
Indeed, failure of either strict inequality gives a one-period arbitrage, while under the strict inequalities the probability
$$
\boxed{q=Q(Y_t=U)=\frac{R-D}{U-D}=\frac{1-d}{u-d}}
$$
lies in $(0,1)$. Taking the moves independent under $Q$ with up probability $q$ makes the discounted stock a <martingale>, since $qu+(1-q)d=1$. This is the unique <risk-neutral probability in the Cox--Ross--Rubinstein model>, hence the unique equivalent martingale measure.

= b
{parent=29k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The arbitrage-free value of the discounted claim is the <conditional expectation>
$$
V_t=\mathbb E_Q[H\mid\mathcal F_t].
$$
Conditional on the observed path $(S_0^1,\ldots,S_t^1)=(x_0,\ldots,x_t)$, the future multiplicative increments are independent of $\mathcal F_t$ and have the same law as a fresh stock path divided by its initial value. Therefore
$$
\boxed{V_t(\omega)=v_t(S_0^1,S_1^1(\omega),\ldots,S_t^1(\omega)),}
$$
where
$$
\boxed{
v_t(x_0,\ldots,x_t)=
\mathbb E_Q\left[h\left(x_0,\ldots,x_t,
x_t\frac{S_1^1}{S_0^1},\ldots,
x_t\frac{S_{T-t}^1}{S_0^1}\right)\right].}
$$
This is <risk-neutral valuation in the Cox--Ross--Rubinstein model> for a path-dependent claim.

= c
{parent=29k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $N=T-t$ and use the discounted multipliers $u,d$. Under $Q$, the number $K$ of up moves in the remaining $N$ periods has the <binomial distribution> $\operatorname{Binomial}(N,q)$. Since
$$
S_T^1=S_t^1u^Kd^{N-K},
$$
the conditional expectation in part (b) becomes
$$
\boxed{
V_t=\sum_{k=0}^{T-t}\binom{T-t}{k}q^k(1-q)^{T-t-k}
h\left(S_t^1u^kd^{T-t-k}\right).}
$$
For an undiscounted stock, replace $u,d$ by $U,D$ and discount the resulting payoff by the appropriate power of $R$.

= d
{parent=29k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Given the present state $(S_t^1,M_t)=(x,m)$, take an independent fresh copy of the stock path and denote its initial value, terminal value after $T-t$ steps, and running maximum by $(y,z,w)=(S_0^1,S_{T-t}^1,M_{T-t})$. Scaling the fresh path to start at $x$ makes its terminal value $xz/y$ and its future running maximum $xw/y$. Including the maximum already attained gives
$$
M_T=\max\left\{m,\frac{xw}{y}\right\}.
$$
Hence define
$$
\boxed{g(x,m,y,z,w)=h\left(\frac{xz}{y},
\max\left\{m,\frac{xw}{y}\right\}\right).}
$$
Independent increments then give the <running-maximum state reduction in the Cox--Ross--Rubinstein model>
$$
\boxed{V_t(\omega)=v_t(S_t^1(\omega),M_t(\omega)),}
$$
with
$$
\boxed{v_t(x,m)=\mathbb E_Q[g(x,m,S_0^1,S_{T-t}^1,M_{T-t})].}
$$

= 30A
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Asymptotic Methods}

= a
{parent=30a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Between the <classical turning points>, $q<0$, while boundedness selects exponentially decaying <WKB approximation> solutions in the forbidden regions $x<a$ and $x>b$. The <Airy turning-point connection formula> at the left turning point matches the left decaying solution to
$$
\psi_L(x)\sim C_L|q(x)|^{-1/4}
\sin\left(\frac1\epsilon\int_a^x|q(\xi)|^{1/2}\,d\xi+\frac\pi4\right)
$$
in the allowed region. Matching the right decaying solution across $b$ gives
$$
\psi_R(x)\sim C_R|q(x)|^{-1/4}
\sin\left(\frac1\epsilon\int_x^b|q(\xi)|^{1/2}\,d\xi+\frac\pi4\right).
$$
For these expressions to be proportional throughout $(a,b)$, their phases must be compatible. Each simple turning point contributes a phase shift $\pi/4$, giving the <two-turning-point WKB quantization condition>
$$
\boxed{
\frac1\epsilon\int_a^b|q(\xi)|^{1/2}\,d\xi
=\left(n+\frac12\right)\pi,
\qquad n=0,1,2,\ldots.}
$$

= b
{parent=30a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $r=e^x$, $\epsilon=\hbar/\sqrt{2m}$, $L=l+1/2$, and $\lambda=-|\lambda|$. Since $V(r)=-V_0/r$, the transformed equation has
$$
q(x)=|\lambda|e^{2x}-V_0e^x+\epsilon^2L^2.
$$
The two turning radii $a<b$ are the positive roots of
$$
|\lambda|r^2-V_0r+\epsilon^2L^2
=|\lambda|(r-a)(r-b),
$$
so
$$
a+b=\frac{V_0}{|\lambda|},
\qquad
ab=\frac{\epsilon^2L^2}{|\lambda|}.
$$
Inside the allowed interval,
$$
|q|=|\lambda|(r-a)(b-r),
\qquad dx=\frac{dr}{r}.
$$
The <semiclassical action integral> is therefore
$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac1\epsilon\int_{x_a}^{x_b}|q(x)|^{1/2}\,dx
&=\frac{\sqrt{|\lambda|}}{\epsilon}
\int_a^b\frac{\sqrt{(r-a)(b-r)}}r\,dr\\
&=\frac{\pi\sqrt{|\lambda|}}{2\epsilon}(\sqrt b-\sqrt a)^2\\
&=\pi\left(\frac{V_0}{2\epsilon\sqrt{|\lambda|}}-L\right).
\end{aligned}
$$
Equating this with $(n+1/2)\pi$ from part (a), and using $L=l+1/2$, gives
$$
\frac{V_0}{2\epsilon\sqrt{|\lambda|}}=n+l+1.
$$
Since $\epsilon^2=\hbar^2/(2m)$,
$$
|\lambda|=\frac{V_0^2}{4\epsilon^2(n+l+1)^2}
=\frac{mV_0^2}{2\hbar^2(n+l+1)^2}.
$$
Thus the <Coulomb WKB quantization with the Langer correction> yields
$$
\boxed{E=\lambda=-\frac{mV_0^2}{2\hbar^2(n+l+1)^2}.}
$$

= 31E
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Dynamical Systems}

= Solution
{parent=31E}

A <fixed point> satisfies
$$
x+y^2-a=0,
\qquad
y(4x-x^2-a)=0.
$$
The branch with $y=0$ is
$$
P_0(a)=(a,0).
$$
Every other fixed point can be parametrized by its $x$-coordinate:
$$
a=4x-x^2,
\qquad
y=\pm\sqrt{x(3-x)},
\qquad 0\leq x\leq3.
$$
Equivalently,
$$
x_\pm=2\pm\sqrt{4-a},
\qquad y=\pm\sqrt{x_\pm(3-x_\pm)}.
$$
The $x_-$ pair exists for $0\leq a\leq4$, and the $x_+$ pair for $3\leq a\leq4$.

The <Jacobian matrix> is
$$
J(x,y)=
\begin{pmatrix}
1&2y\\
y(4-2x)&4x-x^2-a
\end{pmatrix}.
$$
On $P_0(a)$ its <eigenvalues> are $1$ and $a(3-a)$, so this branch has a <nonhyperbolic equilibrium> at $a=0$ and $a=3$. On a nonzero-$y$ branch,
$$
\det J=-4y^2(2-x),
$$
which vanishes at $x=2$, hence at $a=4$ and $y=\pm\sqrt2$. The three bifurcation values and locations are therefore
$$
\boxed{
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
a&\text{bifurcation locations}&\text{type suggested by the fixed-point branches}\\ \hline
0&(0,0)&\text{pitchfork},\\
3&(3,0)&\text{pitchfork},\\
4&(2,\sqrt2),(2,-\sqrt2)&\text{saddle-node at each point}.
\end{array}}
$$

For the <center manifold> calculation, append $\dot a=0$ as in the <extended centre manifold for a parameter>.

At $(a,x,y)=(0,0,0)$ put $\mu=a$ and write the centre manifold as $x=h(y,\mu)$. Its <centre-manifold invariance equation> is
$$
h_y\,y(4h-h^2-\mu)=h+y^2-\mu.
$$
It gives $h=\mu-y^2+O(3)$, and hence
$$
\dot y=y(4h-h^2-\mu)=3\mu y-4y^3+O(4).
$$
This is the <pitchfork bifurcation normal form> with stable nonzero centre branches for $\mu>0$: the bifurcation at $a=0$ is **supercritical**.

At $(a,x,y)=(3,3,0)$ put $u=x-3$ and $\mu=a-3$. Then
$$
\dot u=u+y^2-\mu,
\qquad
\dot y=y(-2u-u^2-\mu).
$$
Again $u=h(y,\mu)=\mu-y^2+O(3)$, so
$$
\dot y=-3\mu y+2y^3+O(4).
$$
The nonzero centre branches exist for $\mu>0$ and are unstable in the centre direction, whereas $y=0$ is centre-stable there. Thus the bifurcation at $a=3$ is a **<subcritical pitchfork bifurcation>** with reversed normal-form parameter.

Finally fix $y_0=\pm\sqrt2$ and put
$$
u=x-2,
\qquad v=y-y_0,
\qquad \mu=a-4.
$$
The translated system is
$$
\dot u=u+2y_0v+v^2-\mu,
\qquad
\dot v=-(y_0+v)(u^2+\mu).
$$
The extended centre manifold has
$$
u=-2y_0v+\mu+O(v^2,v\mu,\mu^2),
$$
and therefore
$$
\dot v=-y_0(\mu+8v^2)+O(v^3,\mu v).
$$
This is the <saddle-node bifurcation> normal form. For $\mu<0$ there are two nearby fixed points, which coalesce and disappear at $\mu=0$. The complete reductions are recorded in the <extended centre-manifold reductions of the 2019 Cambridge reflection-symmetric system>.

= 32B
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Principles of Quantum Mechanics}

= Solution
{parent=32B}

The <spin raising operator> and <spin lowering operator> are
$$
S_+=S_x+iS_y,
\qquad
S_-=S_x-iS_y.
$$
The <angular momentum commutation relations> imply
$$
[S_z,S_\pm]=\pm\hbar S_\pm,
\qquad
[S^2,S_\pm]=0,
$$
so $S_\pm|s,\sigma\rangle$ is proportional to $|s,\sigma\pm1\rangle$. Since
$$
S_\mp S_\pm=S^2-S_z^2\mp\hbar S_z,
$$
its squared <norm> is
$$
\begin{aligned}
\|S_\pm|s,\sigma\rangle\|^2
&=\langle s,\sigma|S_\mp S_\pm|s,\sigma\rangle\\
&=\hbar^2\{s(s+1)-\sigma(\sigma\pm1)\}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Choosing the conventional positive phase gives
$$
\boxed{
S_\pm|s,\sigma\rangle
=\hbar\sqrt{s(s+1)-\sigma(\sigma\pm1)}
\,|s,\sigma\pm1\rangle.}
$$

Using the <spin ladder operators>,
$$
\mathbf S^{(1)}\mathbin\cdot\mathbf S^{(2)}
=S_z^{(1)}S_z^{(2)}
+\frac12\left(S_+^{(1)}S_-^{(2)}
+S_-^{(1)}S_+^{(2)}\right),
$$
and therefore
$$
H=\alpha S_z^{(1)}S_z^{(2)}
+\frac\alpha2\left(S_+^{(1)}S_-^{(2)}
+S_-^{(1)}S_+^{(2)}\right)
+B\left(S_z^{(1)}-S_z^{(2)}\right).
$$
In the ordered product basis of the <tensor product of quantum systems>
$$
|\uparrow\uparrow\rangle,\quad
|\uparrow\downarrow\rangle,\quad
|\downarrow\uparrow\rangle,\quad
|\downarrow\downarrow\rangle,
$$
the <matrix representation> is
$$
H=
\begin{pmatrix}
\alpha\hbar^2/4&0&0&0\\
0&-\alpha\hbar^2/4+B\hbar&\alpha\hbar^2/2&0\\
0&\alpha\hbar^2/2&-\alpha\hbar^2/4-B\hbar&0\\
0&0&0&\alpha\hbar^2/4
\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The two parallel-spin states are already <eigenvectors>. Diagonalizing the central block gives the four <energy eigenvalues>
$$
\boxed{
E_{\uparrow\uparrow}=E_{\downarrow\downarrow}
=\frac{\alpha\hbar^2}{4},
\qquad
E_\pm=-\frac{\alpha\hbar^2}{4}
\pm\sqrt{B^2\hbar^2+\frac{\alpha^2\hbar^4}{4}}.}
$$
This is the spectrum of the <two-spin Heisenberg Hamiltonian in an opposing longitudinal field>.

The asserted ground-state ordering requires the antiferromagnetic case $\alpha>0$. As $B\to0$,
$$
E_-=-\frac{3\alpha\hbar^2}{4},
\qquad
E_+=E_{\uparrow\uparrow}=E_{\downarrow\downarrow}
=\frac{\alpha\hbar^2}{4}.
$$
The corresponding state at $E_-$ is the unique <spin-one-half singlet state>
$$
|0,0\rangle
=\frac{|\uparrow\downarrow\rangle-|\downarrow\uparrow\rangle}{\sqrt2},
$$
whereas the three states at the first excited energy form the <spin-one-half triplet state>.

Indeed, for the <total angular momentum operator>
$$
\mathbf S=\mathbf S^{(1)}+\mathbf S^{(2)},
$$
one has at $B=0$
$$
H=\frac\alpha2\left(
S^2-(S^{(1)})^2-(S^{(2)})^2
\right).
$$
The Hamiltonian then has <rotational symmetry>, so energy depends only on the <total-spin sector>. The singlet has total spin $s=0$ and multiplicity one; the triplet has $s=1$ and multiplicity $2s+1=3$. This rotational symmetry explains why the triplet <energy eigenspace> has dimension three.

= 33B
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Applications of Quantum Mechanics}

= a
{parent=33b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

In the classical experiment, particles whose <impact parameter> lies between $b$ and $b+db$ occupy an incident annulus of area
$$
d\sigma=2\pi b\,|db|.
$$
For a <spherically symmetric potential>, azimuthal symmetry scatters this annulus into the solid-angle strip
$$
d\Omega=2\pi\sin\theta\,|d\theta|.
$$
Thus the <central-potential classical differential cross-section> is
$$
\boxed{\frac{d\sigma}{d\Omega}
=\frac{b}{\sin\theta}
\left|\frac{db}{d\theta}\right|.}
$$
If the relation between $b$ and $\theta$ has several branches, one sums this expression over every branch. The corresponding diagram consists of an incoming line offset by $b$ from the centre, an outgoing asymptote making angle $\theta$ with the incident direction, and the annulus and solid-angle strip just described.

For quantum scattering by a <central potential>, the far-field <wavefunction> is
$$
\psi(\mathbf r)\sim e^{ikz}
+f(\theta)\frac{e^{ikr}}r,
\qquad r\to\infty,
$$
where $f(\theta)$ is the <scattering amplitude>. The incident plane wave has <probability current>
$$
\mathbf J_{\rm in}=\frac{\hbar k}{m}\,\widehat{\mathbf z}.
$$
To leading order in $1/r$, the scattered spherical wave has radial current
$$
\mathbf J_{\rm sc}
=\frac{\hbar k}{m}\frac{|f(\theta)|^2}{r^2}
\,\widehat{\mathbf r}.
$$
The scattered rate through area $r^2d\Omega$ divided by the incident flux is therefore the <quantum differential cross-section from a scattering amplitude>:
$$
\boxed{\frac{d\sigma}{d\Omega}=|f(\theta)|^2.}
$$

= b
{parent=33b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $u=kr-l\pi/2$. Since
$$
A_l\sin u-B_l\cos u
=\frac{-iA_l-B_l}{2}e^{iu}
+\frac{iA_l-B_l}{2}e^{-iu},
$$
the outgoing-to-incoming coefficient ratio is $-S_l$. Hence the <partial-wave S-matrix> element and <scattering phase shift> are
$$
\boxed{
S_l(k)=\frac{A_l(k)-iB_l(k)}{A_l(k)+iB_l(k)}
=e^{2i\delta_l(k)},
\qquad
\tan\delta_l(k)=-\frac{B_l(k)}{A_l(k)}.}
$$
The <scattering length from a partial-wave S-matrix> is
$$
\boxed{
a_s=-\lim_{k\to0}\frac{\tan\delta_0(k)}k
=\lim_{k\to0}\frac{B_0(k)}{kA_0(k)}.}
$$

For the stated <S-wave scattering> solution, $\tanh(\lambda r)\to1$ as $r\to\infty$, so
$$
\psi(r)\sim\frac{e^{-ikr}}r
+\frac{k+i\lambda}{k-i\lambda}\frac{e^{ikr}}r.
$$
Using the same incoming/outgoing convention,
$$
\boxed{
S_0(k)=-\frac{k+i\lambda}{k-i\lambda}
=\frac{\lambda-ik}{\lambda+ik}.}
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{
\delta_0(k)=-\tan^{-1}\frac{k}{\lambda}\pmod\pi,
\qquad
a_s=\frac1\lambda.}
$$

The <analytic continuation> of $S_0$ has a <bound-state pole of the scattering amplitude> at $k=i\lambda$ in the upper half-plane. Since $E=\hbar^2k^2/(2m)$, this gives
$$
\boxed{E_{\rm bound}=-\frac{\hbar^2\lambda^2}{2m}.}
$$
To extract the bound-state wavefunction, multiply the scattering solution by $k-i\lambda$ and take the pole limit:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\psi_{\rm bound}(r)
&\propto i\lambda[1+\tanh(\lambda r)]e^{-\lambda r}/r\\
&\propto \frac{\operatorname{sech}(\lambda r)}r.
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus the unnormalized exterior solution is
$$
\boxed{\psi_{\rm bound}(r)\propto
\frac{\operatorname{sech}(\lambda r)}r,\qquad r>r_0.}
$$
These data are collected in the <hyperbolic-tangent S-wave exterior solution>.

= 34D
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Statistical Physics}

= i
{parent=34d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

<Landau theory> represents the appropriate thermodynamic potential as an analytic function of a small <order parameter>, retaining every low-order term allowed by the system's <symmetry>. At fixed control parameters, the equilibrium phase is the global minimum of the <Landau free energy>; local minima describe metastable phases. A <continuous phase transition> occurs when a minimum changes continuously and loses stability, whereas a <first-order phase transition> occurs when distinct minima exchange global stability.

For
$$
G(\phi)=\frac14\phi^4+\frac12\varepsilon\phi^2,
$$
the stationary points satisfy
$$
G'(\phi)=\phi(\phi^2+\varepsilon)=0.
$$
If $\varepsilon>0$, $\phi=0$ is the unique minimum. If $\varepsilon<0$, $\phi=0$ is a maximum and the two degenerate minima are
$$
\phi_\pm=\pm\sqrt{-\varepsilon},
\qquad
G(\phi_\pm)=-\frac{\varepsilon^2}{4}.
$$
Thus the reflection symmetry $\phi\mapsto-\phi$ is spontaneously broken below $\varepsilon=0$, and
$$
\boxed{\text{the transition at }\varepsilon=0\text{ is continuous, with }|\phi|\sim(-\varepsilon)^{1/2}.}
$$

= ii
{parent=34d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For the <sextic even Landau potential>
$$
G(\phi)=\frac16\phi^6+\frac14g\phi^4+\frac12\varepsilon\phi^2,
$$
put $q=\phi^2$. The stationary-point equation is
$$
G'(\phi)=\phi(q^2+gq+\varepsilon)=0,
$$
so the nonzero stationary points have
$$
q_\pm=\frac{-g\pm\sqrt{g^2-4\varepsilon}}2.
$$
At such a point, $\varepsilon=-q^2-gq$, and hence
$$
G=-\frac13q^3-\frac14gq^2
=-q^2\left(\frac q3+\frac g4\right).
$$
A first-order transition occurs when a nonzero minimum has the same free energy as the disordered minimum $G(0)=0$. Therefore
$$
q=-\frac{3g}{4},
\qquad
\boxed{\varepsilon=\frac{3g^2}{16},\quad g<0.}
$$
At $g=0,\varepsilon=0$, the first-order line meets the continuous line and forms the <tricritical point>. For $g>0$, the transition at $\varepsilon=0$ is continuous.

For $g<0$, the disordered state $\phi=0$ is a local minimum when $\varepsilon>0$ and loses stability at the disordered <spinodal point> $\varepsilon=0$. The ordered minima exist while
$$
g^2-4\varepsilon\geq0,
$$
and merge with the intervening maxima at the ordered spinodal $\varepsilon=g^2/4$. Consequently the two spinodals enclose a region of <metastability>. If increasing $\varepsilon$ corresponds to heating, the ordered phase can persist above the coexistence curve throughout
$$
\boxed{\text{superheating:}\qquad
g<0,\quad \frac{3g^2}{16}<\varepsilon<\frac{g^2}{4},}
$$
while the disordered phase can persist below it throughout
$$
\boxed{\text{supercooling:}\qquad
g<0,\quad 0<\varepsilon<\frac{3g^2}{16}.}
$$

= 35E
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Electrodynamics}

= Solution
{parent=35E}

The <source-free Maxwell equations in a linear medium> are
$$
\boxed{
\nabla\mathbin{\cdot}\mathbf D=0,\qquad
\nabla\mathbin{\cdot}\mathbf B=0,\qquad
\nabla\times\mathbf E=-\frac{\partial\mathbf B}{\partial t},
\qquad
\nabla\times\mathbf H=\frac{\partial\mathbf D}{\partial t}.}
$$
Here the <constitutive relations> are $\mathbf D=\varepsilon\mathbf E$ and $\mathbf B=\mu\mathbf H$.

Substitution of the sinusoidal plane waves into the two divergence equations gives the <transversality of an electromagnetic plane wave>,
$$
\mathbf k\mathbin{\cdot}\mathbf E_0=0,
\qquad
\mathbf k\mathbin{\cdot}\mathbf B_0=0.
$$
<Faraday's law> and the <Ampère-Maxwell equation> give, respectively,
$$
\mathbf k\times\mathbf E_0=\omega\mathbf B_0,
\qquad
\mathbf k\times\mathbf B_0=-\mu\varepsilon\omega\mathbf E_0.
$$
Taking $\mathbf k\times$ the first equation and using transversality yields
$$
-k^2\mathbf E_0
=-\mu\varepsilon\omega^2\mathbf E_0.
$$
Thus a nonzero <plane electromagnetic wave in a linear medium> must satisfy
$$
\boxed{
k^2=\mu\varepsilon\omega^2,\qquad
\mathbf B_0=\frac{\mathbf k\times\mathbf E_0}{\omega},\qquad
v_{\rm ph}=\frac{\omega}{k}=\frac1{\sqrt{\mu\varepsilon}}.}
$$
In particular, $\mathbf E_0$, $\mathbf B_0$, and $\mathbf k$ are mutually orthogonal, with $|\mathbf B_0|=\sqrt{\mu\varepsilon}\,|\mathbf E_0|$.

Take the interface to be $x=0$ and the plane of incidence to be the $xy$-plane. The incident, reflected, and transmitted wavevectors may be written
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf k_I&=k_-(\cos\theta_I,\sin\theta_I,0),\\
\mathbf k_R&=k_-(-\cos\theta_R,\sin\theta_R,0),\\
\mathbf k_T&=k_+(\cos\theta_T,\sin\theta_T,0).
\end{aligned}
$$
The <phase matching at a planar wave interface> requires one common angular frequency and one common tangential wavevector:
$$
k_-\sin\theta_I
=k_-\sin\theta_R
=k_+\sin\theta_T.
$$
Since $k_\pm=\omega\sqrt{\varepsilon_\pm\mu}
=n_\pm\omega/c$, this gives the <Snell law for electromagnetic waves>,
$$
\boxed{\theta_R=\theta_I,\qquad
n_-\sin\theta_I=n_+\sin\theta_T.}
$$

For the final polarization, take the signed electric amplitudes along $\widehat{\mathbf z}$. The <dielectric-interface boundary conditions> require continuity of tangential $\mathbf E$ and $\mathbf H$. Since
$$
\mathbf H=\frac{\mathbf k\times\mathbf E}{\mu\omega},
$$
their $z$- and $y$-components give
$$
E_I+E_R=E_T,
$$
and
$$
k_-\cos\theta_I(E_I-E_R)
=k_+\cos\theta_T E_T.
$$
Eliminating $E_I$ yields
$$
\frac{E_R}{E_T}
=\frac12\left(
1-\frac{k_+\cos\theta_T}{k_-\cos\theta_I}
\right).
$$
Using $\theta_R=\theta_I$ and Snell's law,
$$
\frac{k_+\cos\theta_T}{k_-\cos\theta_I}
=\frac{\sin\theta_R\cos\theta_T}
{\sin\theta_T\cos\theta_R}
=\frac{\tan\theta_R}{\tan\theta_T}.
$$
Therefore the <reflected-to-transmitted transverse-electric amplitude ratio> is
$$
\boxed{
\frac{|\mathbf E_R|}{|\mathbf E_T|}
=\frac12\left(
1-\frac{\tan\theta_R}{\tan\theta_T}
\right),}
$$
with the polarization directions chosen so that the displayed signed factor is nonnegative; otherwise its absolute value gives the magnitude ratio.

= 36D
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=General Relativity}

= a
{parent=36d}
